#
# The predicate census of a closed enactment frontier.
#
# A logic:SaturationWitness earns its name by ENUMERATING: its definition requires
# that the predicates whose extensions the derivation proved final be spelled out
# one by one, because the predicates a witness does not name are exactly the ones
# whose closure it does not certify. "Everything settled" is not a claim anyone can
# falsify; this list is.
#
# So this fixture is the honest form of a closure claim over the enactment kernel:
# a frontier that says it is closed, backed by a witness that names every kernel
# predicate the chase drove to a fixed point, states the budget it was proved
# under, and records that the run finished rather than hitting its bound. Read as
# documentation it answers a question no prose can: which parts of the kernel does
# the frontier derivation actually reach?
#
# It is the positive half of a pair. Its counter-example
# (tests/counter-examples/saturation-census-omits-its-budget.ttl) keeps the whole
# enumeration and drops only the budget, which is the interesting failure: the
# longer the list of settled predicates, the more convincing an unfounded closure
# claim looks.

@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix ex:    <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/examples/logic/tests/> .

ex:censusFrontier a logic:ActionableFrontier ;
    rdfs:label "a frontier whose closure claim names every predicate it settled"@en ;
    logic:frontierSaturationWitness ex:censusSaturation .

ex:censusSaturation a logic:SaturationWitness ;
    rdfs:label "saturation witness for the enactment-kernel chase"@en ;
    logic:resultEvaluation logic:EvaluationCompleted ;
    logic:consumedBudget "steps=41208;pages=96;wallclock-ms=1873" ;
    logic:settledPredicate
